'''''Phlox drummondii''''' (commonly '''annual phlox''' or '''Drummond's phlox''')<ref>[https://plants.usda.gov/java/profile?symbol=PHDR ''P. drummondii'' at USDA Plants Profile]</ref> is a [[flowering plant]] in the genus ''[[Phlox]]'' of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Polemoniaceae. [[native plant|Native]] to Texas, it is also widely distributed in the southeastern United States, especially along public highways. ''P. drummondii'' is often used as an [[ornamental plant]].<ref>[http://www.floridata.com/ref/P/phlo_dru.cfm ''Phlox drummondii'' at Floridata]</ref> The flowers have a wide range of colours "from white and cream through pinks, lilacs, roses, purples and reds, to almost black".<ref>Kelly, James P. 1920. A genetical study of flower form and flower color in ''Phlox drummondii''. Genetics 5(2): 189-248 and 5(3): 361.</ref>

''Phlox drummondii'' is named after Scottish botanist [[Thomas Drummond (botanist)|Thomas Drummond]], who sent it and a variety of other plant samples back to Britain following his 1833–1835 expedition to Texas.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.wildflower.org/plants/result.php?id_plant=PHDR | title = ''Phlox drummondii'' (Annual phlox) | publisher = wildflower.org}}</ref>

==Description==
It is an [[annual plant|annual]], growing from seed each year.<ref name=RHSAZ>{{cite book|title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants|year=2008|publisher=Dorling Kindersley|location=United Kingdom|isbn=978-1405332965|page=1136}}</ref> The branches have sharp, pointed, lengthy, ciliated leaves with rounded flowers. The flowers are single or double, with lightly scented, flat, star-shaped petals. The flowers mature to {{convert|2.5|cm|0|abbr=on}} in diameter. It tolerates cold weather well, but requires plentiful watering and dislikes drought, wind and heat.<ref name="AsakawaAsakawa2001">{{cite book|author1=Bruce Asakawa|author2=Sharon Asakawa|title=California Gardener's Guide|url=https://archive.org/details/brucesharonasaka00asak|url-access=registration|accessdate=25 November 2011|date=3 September 2001|publisher=Cool Springs Press|isbn=978-1-930604-47-6|pages=[https://archive.org/details/brucesharonasaka00asak/page/34 34]–35}}</ref> The plant attracts [[hummingbirds]] and [[butterflies]],<ref>{{Cite web|title=Phlox drummondii - Plant Finder|url=https://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?taxonid=285429&isprofile=0&|access-date=2022-01-01|website=www.missouribotanicalgarden.org}}</ref> which includes its main pollinator responsible for approximately 80% of pollinator visits, ''[[Battus philenor]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Burgin |first=Grace A. |last2=Bronzo‐Munich |first2=Olivia |last3=Garner |first3=Austin G. |last4=Acevedo |first4=Izzy A. |last5=Hopkins |first5=Robin |date=May 2023 |title=Characterizing each step of pollination in Phlox drummondii reveals that a single butterfly species predominates in the pollinator assemblage |url=https://bsapubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ajb2.16172 |journal=American Journal of Botany |language=en |volume=110 |issue=5 |doi=10.1002/ajb2.16172 |issn=0002-9122|url-access=subscription }}</ref>
